"DO NOT INCREASE TIME PER EFFORT OVER OUR BASELINE" — checked directly on OUR
OWN wall (median ours_ms), not on the ratio, because the ratio can move when the
denominator does:

  every cell at 1 MP and above improved or held:
    2048 e3 t1  125 -> 116 ms   -7.2 %      1024 e3 t1  34 -> 32 ms   -7.4 %
    2048 e3 t8   64 ->  61 ms   -5.4 %      1024 e3 t8  20 -> 20 ms   -4.9 %
    2048 e5 t8  168 -> 163 ms   -3.3 %      1024 e5 t1 112 ->111 ms   -1.3 %
    2048 e7 t8  238 -> 234 ms   -2.1 %      1024 e7 t8  67 -> 66 ms   -1.5 %
  the largest INCREASE anywhere in the grid is +1.9 % (1024 e9 t8, 371 -> 378 ms),
  which is inside the run-to-run band for that cell — e9 t=8 is perceptual-loop
  and thread-scheduling bound and this change does not touch either.

WHERE IT LANDED, AND WHY IT IS WHERE IT IS:
  - **e3 is where the cube root lives.** `convert_rows_to_xyb` is 15.3 % of
    lossy e3 CPU (e3_profile_2026-09-10.md) and the cube root is 86-87 % of that
    transform, so e3 is the band with the most cube root per byte of output.
    It moved most: -7 to -8 % of ours/cjxl at 1 MP and 4 MP.
  - **e3 at >= 1 MP was one of the two open items the `_after_` run named.** It
    goes 1.25 -> 1.17 (1024 t=1) and 1.23 -> 1.14 (2048 t=1), and at t=8 it is
    now AT or BELOW parity (1.00 and 0.88). The other open item, e5 at t=8, is
    barely touched (1.32 -> 1.30 at 2048) and is now the loosest cell in the
    grid by a clear margin — that band is `acstrat`-bound, not XYB-bound.
  - **<= 256 px is flat within noise (+-2 %), as it should be.** At 4-6 ms the
    encode is dominated by per-call fixed cost; a per-pixel kernel has nothing
    to bite on. Do not read the flat 64/256 rows as a failed change.
  - **e9 is flat.** The perceptual loop re-runs the transform per iteration but
    is dominated by the metric, not by XYB.

ATTRIBUTION, STATED HONESTLY: this grid measures the cube-root change and the
hand-written NEON tier TOGETHER and cannot separate them by itself. The separate
in-process A/B (xyb_neon_handwritten_2026-09-10.{tsv,meta}) puts the NEON tier
at a WASH on the `LowP` path that zen ships — median 0.989 over 10 runs — so
effectively all of the movement above is the cube root. Do not credit the NEON
kernel for it.

BYTES: the cube-root change moves them; the NEON tier does not (byte-identical).
Over the 112 (image, effort, distance) cells:

  median +0.000 %   mean +0.018 %   50 of 112 byte-IDENTICAL
  p25 -0.002 %      p75 +0.006 %
  min  -0.972 % (nature_256 e9 d1.0, 8437 -> 8355 B)
  max  +2.387 % (nature_64  e9 d4.0,  419 ->  429 B — ten bytes on a 64x64)

  **Every moved cell is at e9**, which is the expected shape: e9 runs the
  perceptual loop, so a sub-ULP change in the XYB planes changes where the loop
  converges. e3/e5/e7 are byte-identical throughout. The one real-size increase
  worth naming is brochure_2048 e9 d1.0, 132340 -> 134420 B (+1.57 %); the
  quality side of that trade was measured separately at the cube-root commit
  (60 cells across all 21 imazen-26 strata: SSIM2 delta median +0.0005, min
  -0.0517, max +0.1886).

STILL OPEN, in priority order, unchanged except that item 1 has moved:
  1. **e5 at threads=8** — 1.30 at 2048, 1.11 at 1024. Now the loosest band on
     the ladder by a clear margin. The lever named for it is `acstrat`
     (~40 % of e5 t8), which is algorithmic, not a kernel.
  2. **e3 at threads=1** — 1.14 (2048) / 1.17 (1024). Improved but still above
     parity.
  3. e9 t=1 sits at 1.04-1.06 and is perceptual-loop bound.
